<section class="ad2e-manual-layout">
  <nav class="ad2e-manual-nav">
    {{#each sections as |s|}}
    <button type="button" data-action="section" data-section="{{s.id}}" class="{{#if s.active}}active{{/if}}">{{s.label}}</button>
    {{/each}}
    <p class="ad2e-note">{{localize "AD2E.Manual.Version"}} {{version}}</p>
  </nav>
  <div class="ad2e-manual-body">

  {{#if is.start}}
  <h2>Getting started</h2>
  <p>This system runs the mechanics of AD&amp;D 2nd Edition: characters, monsters, combat, spells, treasure and the optional
  rules listed under <em>Optional rules</em>. It holds no rulebook text: item sheets link to the source page instead, so keep
  the books (or the AD&amp;D 2e wiki) at hand.</p>
  <h3>First steps for a new world</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Check the world settings (<em>Settings</em> section): death rule, initiative method, encumbrance rule and the optional rules.</li>
    <li>Import the monsters you need (<em>GM tools</em> &gt; Import monsters). Their pictures are copied into the world folder <code>ad2e-monsters</code>.</li>
    <li>Import spells beyond the 12 examples (<em>GM tools</em> &gt; Import spells); they go into the world compendium "Imported Spells".</li>
    <li>Create characters: drag a race, then a class, then a kit from the compendiums onto the sheet (<em>Characters</em> section).</li>
    <li>After a system update that changes imported data, run <em>Update existing monsters</em> and <em>Update existing spells</em>.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Compendiums of this system</h3>
  <ul class="ad2e-manual-columns">
    {{#each packs as |p|}}<li>{{p.label}} <span class="ad2e-weapon-meta">({{p.type}})</span></li>{{/each}}
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.tools}}
  <h2>GM tools</h2>
  {{#unless isGM}}<p class="ad2e-unmet">{{localize "AD2E.Manual.GmOnly"}}</p>{{/unless}}
  <div class="ad2e-manual-tools">
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="importMonsters"><i class="fa-solid fa-dragon"></i> Import monsters</button>
      <p>Choose a setting and source book from completecompendium.com, select stat blocks and import them as Monster / NPC
      actors (stat block, link to the page, picture as portrait and token; token size from the size letter: T ½, S and M 1,
      L 2, H 3, G 4 squares). Importing the same stat block again updates the actor.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="updateMonsters"><i class="fa-solid fa-rotate"></i> Update existing monsters</button>
      <p>Re-reads the page of every imported monster in the world and in unlocked world compendiums. Names, current hit
      points, pictures and token sizes you set, and attack elements you chose stay; tokens already placed on scenes get the
      new picture and size if they still had the old one.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="importSpells"><i class="fa-solid fa-wand-sparkles"></i> Import spells</button>
      <p>Choose a source book from the AD&amp;D 2e wiki, filter by class and level, and import the spells' statistics and links
      into the world compendium "Imported Spells" (folders by class and level). Material components are linked to the Spell
      Components compendium, and damage or healing dice are read for the spell's Damage button. After each import the
      compendium is read back from the server: if it is missing or lacks any imported spell, an error replaces the success
      message (details in the browser console, F12).</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="updateSpells"><i class="fa-solid fa-rotate"></i> Update existing spells</button>
      <p>Refreshes every spell in the Items directory, on characters and in "Imported Spells" from its wiki page; memorization,
      learned status and notes stay.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="awardXp"><i class="fa-solid fa-star"></i> Award experience</button>
      <p>Divides an experience award equally among the chosen characters; each share gets that character's prime requisite bonus. A single
      character's award is the "+" next to XP on the sheet. When you end a combat with monsters in it, this window opens by
      itself (world setting) with the dead monsters ticked and the combat's characters as recipients; tick monsters that
      fled or surrendered too. A monster with no XP value is calculated from its Hit Dice (marked "calculated"). Warriors
      (10 XP) and bards (5 XP) can also get the DMG's class award per Hit Die of the creatures defeated: untick it for
      anyone who did not earn it.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createMonster"><i class="fa-solid fa-dragon"></i> Create monster</button>
      <p>A guided window for a new Monster / NPC actor: size, intelligence and morale with their Monstrous Manual bands,
      Hit Dice, AC, movement and attacks. THAC0 and saving throws follow the Hit Dice; experience is DMG Table 31 for the
      Hit Dice plus the Table 32 special abilities you tick (you may enter your own value). Hit points are rolled. The
      actor goes into the world or an unlocked world compendium, and its sheet opens for anything else.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createWeapon"><i class="fa-solid fa-khanda"></i> Create weapon</button>
      <p>A guided window for a new weapon item: start from any weapon in the compendiums or the Items directory, or a blank
      one, and set its size, type, speed factor, damage (two rows, e.g. one- and two-handed), melee and missile use, rate
      of fire and range, Strength use, magical bonuses and notes; these statistics are read-only on the weapon sheet. It can
      create a matching weapon proficiency, or link an existing one so characters proficient with it use it.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createItem"><i class="fa-solid fa-sack-dollar"></i> Create item</button>
      <p>A guided window for equipment (with its category), a container (capacity in pounds or volume), armour (body armour
      AC, a shield's bonus against melee and missiles and the attackers it covers, a helmet; the size it was made for and a
      magical bonus) or ammunition (the weapons that fire it and its damage). Start from an existing item of that kind or a
      blank one; the cost is checked ("5 gp", "2 sp each").</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createMagicItem"><i class="fa-solid fa-hat-wizard"></i> Create magic item</button>
      <p>Either a magical item (category, charges when found for wands, rods and staves, rolled at once if you like, the
      classes that may use it, XP and gp values, a container's capacity and whether its contents weigh nothing), or
      magical arms: a copy of a weapon, armour or ammunition with a bonus (-5 to +5), named and valued from the DMG tables
      (armour +1 500 XP, swords +1 400 XP, other weapons +1 500 XP and so on; the value goes in the notes). Items are
      unidentified unless you tick Identified.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createPatron"><i class="fa-solid fa-crown"></i> Create patron</button>
      <p>An NPC who hires or sponsors the party, as a Monster / NPC actor with the role "Patron". Choose race, class group
      and level (THAC0, saving throws and hit points follow; 0-level is possible), alignment, AC and morale; write what the
      patron wants, offers and pays; choose or roll a personality from DMG Table 70 and appearance words from the DMG's NPC
      section. Everything but the numbers goes into the actor's notes.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createSpell"><i class="fa-solid fa-wand-sparkles"></i> Create spell</button>
      <p>A new wizard or priest spell: schools or spheres (the PHB lists, plus any typed), level, components, range, area,
      casting time, duration, save and damage or healing options (rolled from the Spells tab). Advice from the DMG's
      spell research section: the level for the damage dice, research time (two weeks per level, a week more per failed
      check) and cost (100-1,000 gp per level). Choose the researching character to roll the research check (a wizard's
      chance to learn the spell, a priest's Wisdom check); once researched the spell can go straight into their spells.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="createTrap"><i class="fa-solid fa-burst"></i> Create trap</button>
      <p>A trap actor (a token on the map: pits, deadfalls) or a trap item (put on the chest, door or other object it
      guards). It hits by an attack at its THAC0 against the victim's AC, or by the victim's saving throw (avoided or halved
      on a success); damage, trigger, effect and reset; and a find/remove modifier up to 30% either way. The trap's sheet
      (or the item's) has a GM "Spring" button: target the victims' tokens first; the attack or saves are rolled and the
      damage comes with the usual apply buttons. A thief's Find/Remove Traps roll offers the targeted trap's modifier and a
      silent attempt (-10%, a click on 01-10).</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="poisonTargets"><i class="fa-solid fa-skull-crossbones"></i> Poison targets</button>
      <p>Gives a poison (DMG Table 51 class A-P) to the targeted tokens: a drugged drink, a contact poison on a door handle.
      Choose how it is given: a poison given another way than its own works at half strength (injected or ingested
      swapped), and injected or ingested poisons do nothing by contact. A save request goes to chat (see Combat, Poison).</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="falling"><i class="fa-solid fa-person-falling"></i> Falling damage</button>
      <p>Target (or select) the tokens that fell and enter the distance: 1d6 per full 10 feet, at most 20d6, posted with the
      usual apply buttons (no massive-damage check: a fall is not an attack). Item saves vs. Fall follow if ticked (more
      than 5 feet; soft surface +5, -1 per 5 feet beyond the first).</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="itemSaves"><i class="fa-solid fa-flask"></i> Item saving throws</button>
      <p>For the selected (or targeted) tokens: choose the attack form (acid, crushing blow, disintegration, fall, magical
      or normal fire, cold, lightning bolt, electricity) and roll DMG Table 29 for the ticked items. Fragile items
      (potions, oils, paper, glass, pottery) are ticked; adjust material, bonus and the +2 "designed to counter" box per item.
      A failed item is renamed "(destroyed)", unequipped and no longer carried; delete it when ready. The same window opens
      from the "Item saves" button next to a failed save on a spell or poison save card.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="rollTreasure"><i class="fa-solid fa-coins"></i> Roll treasure</button>
      <p>Rolls DMG treasure types (e.g. "Q x5, X"): coins, gems, objects of art and magical items. The result is a GM chat card;
      its button adds everything to the selected token's actor, magical items unidentified. Monster sheets have the same
      button, prefilled from the stat block.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="rollReaction"><i class="fa-solid fa-comments"></i> Encounter reaction</button>
      <p>Rolls the encounter reaction table with the speaker's Charisma and kit reaction modifiers. Monster sheets have the
      same button.</p></div>
    <div><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="settings"><i class="fa-solid fa-gears"></i> Configure Settings</button>
      <p>Foundry's settings window; this system's settings are under its own tab.</p></div>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.settings}}
  <h2>Settings</h2>
  <p>Current values in this world. World settings apply to everyone and only the GM changes them; client settings are per
  user and browser. Change them in Configure Settings.</p>
  <p><button type="button" data-action="tool" data-tool="settings"><i class="fa-solid fa-gears"></i> Configure Settings</button></p>
  <table class="ad2e-manual-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {{#each settings as |s|}}
    <tr><td><strong>{{s.name}}</strong><br><span class="ad2e-weapon-meta">{{s.scope}}{{#if s.reload}}, {{localize "AD2E.Manual.Reload"}}{{/if}}</span></td>
      <td>{{s.value}}</td><td>{{s.hint}}</td></tr>
    {{/each}}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Setting menus</h3>
  <ul>
    {{#each menus as |m|}}
    <li><button type="button" data-action="menu" data-menu="{{m.key}}"><i class="{{m.icon}}"></i> {{m.name}}</button> {{m.hint}}</li>
    {{/each}}
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.characters}}
  <h2>Characters</h2>
  <h3>Creating a character</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter the rolled ability scores on the Abilities panel, or use its dice button (PHB Methods II-VI; exceptional
    Strength for warriors with 18).</li>
    <li>Drag a race from "Races (PHB)". Racial adjustments are added to the rolled scores; the race limits the classes.</li>
    <li>Drag a class from "Classes (PHB)". Minimums, alignment and racial level limits are checked on the Class tab.</li>
    <li>Optionally drag a kit from "Class Kits". A kit that does not fit the class or race is refused or flagged. Kit bonus
    proficiencies are added free; required ones use slots. Kit modifiers apply automatically, or as tick boxes in the roll
    dialogs when they depend on the situation.</li>
    <li>Drag proficiencies from "Proficiencies". Slots used and available are shown; extra slots on a nonweapon proficiency
    add +1 each. Traits and disadvantages (Skills &amp; Powers) are optional; traits must be paid for with disadvantages.</li>
    <li>Equip weapons, armour and gear from the equipment compendiums (Equipped tick box). Coins are items; containers carry items.</li>
    <li>Spellcasters: drop spells on the sheet. Wizards and bards roll to learn; the Spells tab shows slots by level, Memorize
    (-/+), Cast and Rest. The Spells tab is hidden for characters without spells.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Age</h3>
  <p>On the Bio tab, "Roll" rolls the starting age (PHB Table 11: base + dice for the race); your own field "Maximum age
  (GM)" and its Roll button are hidden from players, and you are whispered when the character reaches it (elves leave
  rather than die). The ageing changes of PHB Table 12 (middle age, old, venerable) are applied to the scores from the Age
  and race, cumulatively; exceptional Strength is halved at middle age and lost (with 1 point) when old. Magical
  longevity changes the maximum age, not the age.</p>
  <h3>Advancing</h3>
  <p>"Level Up" rolls hit points; saving throws, THAC0, slots, thief skills and spells follow the level. Thief and bard skill
  points are spent on the Class Abilities tab. Henchmen: drag an actor onto the sheet (Bio tab). Mounts, pack animals and
  pets: drag the animal actor onto the sheet; they are listed on the Equipment tab with their load, and "Riding" marks the
  one the character rides (its load then includes the rider).</p>
  <h3>Followers and strongholds</h3>
  <p>On the Bio tab, "Followers &amp; stronghold" records the stronghold (name, kind, Built). Each class with followers shows
  the level it needs: fighter 9 and bard 9 (a built stronghold), cleric 8 (a built place of worship), ranger 10 and thief
  10. When a class is ready, the GM's "Attract followers" button rolls once (Table 16, 19 or 31, or the number of
  0-level soldiers; cleric 2d10 x 10), creates one Monster / NPC actor per unit (role "Follower unit", "Figures in unit"
  = how many it stands for, hit points of one figure) and posts a GM-only card. Equipment without a compendium item and
  creatures without statistics are noted on the actor with a link. Units dropped on the sheet join the list; removing one
  keeps the actor. Paladins attract no followers.</p>
  <h3>Dual-class characters</h3>
  <p>With the world setting "Dual-class characters" on, dropping a new class on a character who has one asks whether to
  dual-class or replace the class (dual-class only for eligible humans: prime requisites 15+ in the current class and 17+
  in the new one, at least 2nd level). The earlier class stays with its abilities and spells at its last level. Until the
  new class's level is higher, the earlier class's abilities, THAC0 or saves can still be used but set the experience
  penalty: experience awards then ask "encounter" (no experience) or "adventure" (half). The Race &amp; Class tab shows
  the status and has buttons to clear the penalty, and "Undo dual-class" to correct a mistaken switch: the current class
  is removed and the earlier one comes back from the Classes compendium at its last level, with the experience it had at
  the switch (for switches made before 0.0.122, the minimum for that level). Hit points and the kit are not changed.</p>
  <h3>Multi-class characters</h3>
  <p>With the world setting "Multi-class characters" on, dropping another class on a demihuman asks whether to add it
  (multi-class) or replace the class. Adding is offered at creation only, for the race's combinations, with an allowed
  alignment and the class's minimums. Experience awards (individual and group) are divided between the classes, each
  share with its class's prime requisite bonus. The Race &amp; Class tab lists the classes with a Level Up button each; a
  class stops at its racial level limit. After adding the classes, roll 1st-level hit points again (the dice are
  averaged). One kit, open to any of the classes; kits from the Complete Fighter's and Thief's Handbooks show a
  single-class warning for you to rule on. Bard combinations from the Complete Bard's Handbook need one of the kits the
  Race &amp; Class tab names. The other classes' levels and experience can be corrected in the class table. Weapons outside
  a class's list are marked (a priest's list always applies to a multi-class priest; for a specialty priest fill in
  "Allowed weapons" on the class or kit item); a multi-class druid's armour limits always show; casting a wizard spell in armour
  asks for confirmation (elves in elven chain excepted).</p>
  <h3>Skills &amp; Powers kit features</h3>
  <p>Social rank: Roll on the Race &amp; Class tab (the kit's 2d6 table). Encounter reactions ask the NPC's social class
  when a Pugilist is present and whether it is a first meeting with a Barbarian. A Weapon Master's "Display" (Combat
  tab, during combat) worsens the other side's initiative in rounds 1 and 2 (untick it in the dialog for anyone who did
  not see it). A Mystic's "Meditate" boosts one ability after the meditation for a third of its time; advance world time
  to start and end it. Weapon Masters: the chosen weapon is the melee weapon with specialization (else expertise), at no extra cost;
  proficiencies of another weapon type are marked. With the Skills &amp; Powers weapon rules the chosen weapon is also
  the weapon of choice (+1 to hit) at no cost, and the weapon-of-choice box is hidden.</p>
  <h3>Animal companions and mounts (Skills &amp; Powers)</h3>
  <p>Animal Masters and Riders have a bonded animal, shown on the Biography tab. Roll Table 42 or 43 with the button
  there (or let the player choose); for a Rider choose the homeland (settled lands, desert, jungle, forest, mountains,
  underground, coast) so the mount suits it, or "Any" for the whole table and drag the creature from the chat card's link onto the sheet: the sheet offers to
  bond it. The creatures are in Hirelings &amp; Mounts, folder "Animal Companions &amp; Mounts (POSP)". When the animal
  dies, chat tells you; then use "Companion died" or "Companion lost carelessly" (-10% experience, that species never
  again), or "Mount died" (2d6 damage; for negligence a save vs. spells, on a failure the rider is feebleminded for the 2d6 hours: a status icon and a note on the Biography tab,
  casting asks to confirm, it ends when world time passes or with "End feeblemind")
  and "Mount fled" (no bonded mount again). A companion larger than size S is marked on the Biography tab (the kit asks
  for small animals); you decide whether it stays. Warnings (yours to decide) also appear on the table roll, the
  bonding prompt and the Biography tab when a mount is too big (gnome, halfling) or too small (medium races) for the
  rider's race, when a companion is outside an Animal Master's race affinity (dwarf and gnome: burrowing animals; elf:
  forest animals), or when its good/evil alignment opposes the master's. Cavaliers and Nobles without a mount get a warning on the Race &amp; Class
  tab.</p>
  <h3>Energy drain and restoration</h3>
  <p>The Main tab of a character has two GM buttons. "Energy drain" asks how many levels are lost: each comes off the
  highest class (multi- and dual-class characters), reduces maximum hit points by that level's roll, and sets that
  class's experience halfway to the next level; wizard spells the character can no longer cast are marked not
  understood. If memorized spells exceed the new slots, a dialog asks which memorizations are forgotten (uncast ones
  are proposed). A character drained below 1st level becomes 0-level and cannot level up; a further drain kills the
  character, and you are whispered how many days (2d4) until the character rises as an undead of its slayer's type.
  "Restoration" brings back the most recent drained level (with its hit points and the minimum experience for the
  level) if cast in time: one day per level of the priest, which the dialog asks for; choose the casting priest there
  and both recorded ages (Biography tab) go up by two years. Drained levels are listed on the Main tab until
  regained.</p>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.combat}}
  <h2>Combat</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Initiative</strong>: 1d10, lowest first. Rolling one combatant from the tracker asks for its action (weapon
    speed, spell casting time, magical item, natural weapons) and situation; Roll All uses each combatant's weapon in hand.
    The world setting chooses individual, group or standard (one roll per side; sides are token dispositions).</li>
    <li><strong>Attacks</strong>: from the Combat tab (characters) or the Attacks table (monsters). Target tokens first: the
    target's AC fills in, and status icons tick the combat modifiers (prone, stunned, invisible, held or asleep). Two
    weapons, unarmed attacks (punch, wrestle, overbear), mounted combat and missile ranges are options in the dialogs.</li>
    <li><strong>Damage</strong>: rolled automatically on a hit (client setting), or with the Damage button. The GM applies it
    with the buttons under the chat message (to the roll's targets, the GM's targets or the selected tokens) or the message's
    right-click menu. Elemental mages and gens adjust damage of their element there.</li>
    <li><strong>Hit points and death</strong>: the world death rule chooses death at 0 or the "death's door" option
    (unconscious at 0, bleeding each round until wounds are bound, dead at -10). Sheet buttons: Damage, Heal, Rest,
    Bind wounds, Raise. Punching damage and temporary damage return on their own.</li>
    <li><strong>Surprise, morale, reactions</strong>: Surprise on the character sheet and the eye button on monster
    sheets; morale and reaction buttons on monster sheets.</li>
    <li><strong>Modifiers</strong>: every roll dialog has a modifier and reason for anything the system does not track.</li>
    <li><strong>Lasso</strong>: a character attacking with a lasso gets its own dialog: legs (pull/trip), arms (trap),
    unhorse a rider, or a pull/trip by spurring a mount the lasso is tied to. Target the defender first: its Strength,
    Dexterity, THAC0 and size are filled in (for monsters from size, Hit Dice and movement) and can be changed. Tick four
    legs, unaware or stationary as they apply. Chat shows every roll and the result.</li>
    <li><strong>Net</strong>: attacking with a net opens its own dialog: throw (traps weapon and shield; the target AC
    counts only Dexterity and magic, so enter any magical protection), loop the rope round (the victim breaks free at -4),
    pull/trip, or fold the net (2 rounds; after the first throw it is at -4 to hit until folded). A netted character or
    monster uses "Break free (net)" (Combat tab, or the monster's header): a Strength check.</li>
    <li><strong>Poison</strong>: a monster's natural attack can carry a DMG Table 51 poison class (Attacks table, Poison
    column); weapons and missiles are coated from their sheet with a poison item (compendium "Poisons (DMG)", one dose per
    weapon or missile); traps have a poison class and how they deliver it. A damage roll with a poisoned attack posts a
    save request vs. poison: each target's owner clicks Save, or you roll them all (dwarves and halflings add their
    Constitution bonus vs. poison). Once a save is in, the system rolls the onset (whispered to you) and applies the
    effect when game time reaches it: damage, death, paralysis (2d6 hours) or debilitation (1d3 days: ability scores and
    movement halved, no healing). Advance game time (the round count in combat does it) for the onset to come. The sheet
    shows pending poison; your "Neutralize poison" button ends it (hit points already lost stay lost). A GM must be
    connected for saves to be recorded. Coated weapons lose their dose on each damage roll (implementation choice).</li>
    <li><strong>Spells, magic resistance and saves</strong>: a caster targets the creatures, then clicks Cast. Each target
    with magic resistance rolls d100 at once (resisted at or below its value; monsters use the first percentage in
    their Magic Resistance text, characters the field next to their saves; "Lowered" skips the roll). No caster-level
    adjustment is applied. If the spell's Save entry is not None, the card asks the remaining targets for a save
    (the category is the spell sheet's "Save vs.", spell by default): owners click Save, or you roll them all. The
    spell's Damage roll then goes to those who failed or did not roll, half to those who saved ("1/2"), nothing for
    "Neg.", and for other entries a separate full-damage message for those who saved, to apply as the spell says.
    Area spells: target every creature in the area.</li>
    <li><strong>Class weapons</strong>: weapons outside the class's list are marked in red and the attack dialog warns;
    the attack is still allowed (your ruling).</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.monsters}}
  <h2>Monsters, hirelings, mounts and familiars</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Monster / NPC actors</strong> hold a stat block: Hit Dice as written (with HP rolls), THAC0 from the DMG,
    saving throws by Hit Dice, morale, natural attacks plus owned weapons and armour. "Monsters &amp; NPCs (prototypes)"
    has an orc, a riding horse and a mercenary as examples.</li>
    <li><strong>Hirelings &amp; Mounts</strong> compendium: DMG soldiers with their equipment and wages, civilian
    specialists, horses, camels, an elephant, pack animals, familiars, and the Skills &amp; Powers animal companions and
    mounts (Animal Master Table 42, Rider Table 43; see <em>Characters</em>).</li>
    <li><strong>Mounts</strong>: a rider's load is checked against the mount's capacity; the Push button pushes a mount
    beyond its normal pace. With the world setting on, a rider's and mount's tokens move together.</li>
    <li><strong>Familiars</strong>: Find Familiar is rolled by the GM from the character sheet; the familiar's death and
    separation are tracked. Sha'ir characters summon and send their gen from the sheet.</li>
    <li><strong>Importing</strong>: see <em>GM tools</em>. Pictures are not shipped with the system; the importer copies
    them into the world (setting "Store imported monster pictures in the world").</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.items}}
  <h2>Items, treasure and identification</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Weapons, ammunition, armour, equipment, coins, gems, jewellery and magical items are items on the Equipment tab
    (monsters: Inventory tab). Carried items count toward encumbrance (world setting chooses the encumbrance rule).</li>
    <li><strong>Containers</strong>: drag an item's row onto a container in the Containers list (or choose "In container"
    on the item sheet); contents count while the container is carried, and its capacity is checked.</li>
    <li><strong>Ammunition</strong>: firing a launcher asks which ammunition and spends one.</li>
    <li><strong>Magical items</strong>: "Magical Items (DMG)" lists the DMG tables with XP values and links; wands, rods
    and staves roll their charges; "Use" spends a charge or one potion or scroll.</li>
    <li><strong>Identification</strong>: treasure arrives unidentified. Players see a generic name and no values; only the
    GM ticks Identified on the item sheet. Bonuses still apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Spell components</strong>: with the world setting on, casting a spell uses up its consumed material
    components from the caster's items.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

  {{#if is.optional}}
  <h2>Optional rules</h2>
  <p>Each is a world setting (see <em>Settings</em>); all are off unless noted.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Called shots and attack options</strong>: attack dialogs get a Maneuver select. Called shot: -4 (or -6/-8
    for hard ones), +1 initiative (announce it before initiative), and with System II criticals the location aimed at.
    Sap: -4 (-8 against a helmet, ticked from the target's equipped helmet), Small or Medium targets; the damage is
    punching damage and a knockout chance (5% per point up to 40%, doubled against a helpless victim) is rolled.
    Disarm, grab, trap and block open a second window with the defender's numbers (filled from the target) and roll both
    sides: opposed attack rolls against AC 0 and AC 4 (block: AC 4 against the opponent's attack on you); a disarmed
    weapon's landing spot is rolled; a grab goes on to opposed Strength. Pull/trip (the weapons the rules name, e.g.
    polearms, spears, bows, whips) and shield-punch / shield-rush (an equipped shield) roll the attack and the Strength
    contest. Monsters get called shots, disarm, grab, trap and block.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical hits (Combat &amp; Tactics)</strong>: Off, System I or System II. A natural 18 or more that hits by
    5 or more is a critical for characters and monsters alike. System I doubles the damage dice (added to other
    multipliers: a charging lance does 3d6). System II also rolls hit location and severity for the chart of the
    weapon's type against the target's kind (characters are humanoids; set a monster's "Critical hit chart" in its
    combat panel, default monster) and posts a save vs. death request: on a failed save open the linked chart for the
    injury and apply it yourself (the injury texts are not copied into the system). Severity 13+ triples the damage
    dice. The Damage dialog shows the critical, pre-set from the attack, so you can change it.</li>
    <li><strong>Weapon type vs. armour (PHB Table 52)</strong>: an attack against a target wearing body armour from the
    table adds its modifier for the weapon's best damage type (a halberd uses slashing or piercing, whichever is
    better). Natural Armor Classes get none. Monster natural attacks need a type (S, P, B) in the Attacks table. The
    modifier shows in the attack's chat line.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills &amp; Powers weapon rules</strong>: weapon groups, fighting styles, armour and shield proficiencies,
    weapon of choice, expertise, specialization for every class and mastery. Character points are not used: costs are
    paid with weapon slots.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills &amp; Powers nonweapon proficiency ratings</strong>: Table 45 ratings with the Table 44 ability
    modifier instead of the PHB check; extra slots add +1, up to 16 before ability modifiers.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills &amp; Powers kits and traits</strong> are always available as items (no setting): the Skills &amp; Powers
    kits folder and the "Traits &amp; Disadvantages" compendium.</li>
    <li><strong>Combat &amp; Tactics weapons</strong> are items in the weapon compendiums (misfires, knockdown dice, firearms
    powder and matches).</li>
    <li><strong>Dual-class</strong> and <strong>multi-class characters</strong>: see <em>Characters</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Al-Qadim</strong>: kits, sha'ir and gens, elemental mages; the heat penalty for heavy armour is a setting.</li>
    <li><strong>Spell components</strong>, <strong>maximum spells per level</strong> and the <strong>initiative question</strong>
    are settings too.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}

